  {
    "equipment_id": "1830",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 1830",
    "equipment_name": "Staple Gun",
    "department": "Shipping",
    "wo_id": "15141",
    "date_notified": "03/24/2025",
    "urgency": "6. General Maint",
    "problem": "Good morning Maintenance team,\n\nPer your request I inputted this work order again. We had an injury this past Thursday involving the staple gun, which was reported to have misfired. I\u2019ve removed it from use, and it is currently placed on the empty desk in the shipping office.\n\nBefore it's used again, I\u2019d like maintenance to inspect it. According to the team, this is our newest and best staple gun, so please note that the older ones may not be as reliable in the meantime.\n\nThank you in advance!",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "0.5",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "03/21/2025 01:00 PM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 03/24/25  5:38:02 AM: I brought the staple gun to maintenance. Inspected all parts before hooking up to air. All parts on the gun are in good shape and nothing has been modified or damaged. I hooked up air and began testing the gun. I attempted any which way I could to get it to fire just by engaging the push lever on the end of the gun. The trigger assembly is working the way it should. Every time it is engaged and disengaged, the gun reacts accordingly. The internal piston is shifting without issues when the trigger is engaged. I cannot get the gun to fire at all without engaging the push lever on the end. The trigger must be pulled and the end push lever must be engaged to drive a staple with these guns. If I dropped the gun, the only way for it to fire a staple is if something pulled the trigger at the same time the push lever is engaged. I couldn't get it to fire a staple by any other way than is designed. There is an attachment that explains the moving parts so this makes more sense. | Preston Ellexson - 03/24/25  5:37:36 AM: I brought the staple gun to maintenance. Inspected all parts before hooking up to air. All parts on the gun are in good shape and nothing has been modified or damaged. I hooked up air and began testing the gun. I attempted any which way I could to get it to fire just by engaging the push lever on the end of the gun. The trigger assembly is working the way it should. Every time it is engaged and disengaged, the gun reacts accordingly. The internal piston is shifting without issues when the trigger is engaged. I cannot get the gun to fire at all without engaging the push lever on the end. The trigger must be pulled and the end push lever must be engaged to drive a staple with these guns. If I dropped the gun, the only way for it to fire a staple is if something pulled the trigger at the same time the push lever is engaged. I couldn't get it to fire a staple by any other way than is designed. There is an attachment that explains the moving parts so this makes more sense.",
    "attachments": [
      {
        "name": "Hitachi Staple Gun.PNG",
        "url": "https://circaweb.bobrick.com/PME/Forms/DownloadAttachment/752?guid=c99c9431-7cf4-4e9f-886d-44127659ff67"
      }
    ]
  },
